Format Specifiers in C

Today, I learned about format specifiers in C programming. 
Format specifiers are special tokens that begin with a ' % ' symbol followed by a character that specifies the data type and optional modifiers (width, precision, flags).
They control how data is displayed or interpreted.
Format specifiers are used with printf() and scanf() functions to display output and take input from the user.

What I Studied
%d – Integer
%f – Float
%lf – Double in scanf()
%c – Character
%s – String
%u – Unsigned integer


Code Example

#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int age = 20;
    float marks = 85.5;
    char grade = 'A';
    char name[] = "Nova";

    printf("Age: %d\n", age);
    printf("Marks: %f\n", marks);
    printf("Grade: %c\n", grade);
    printf("Name: %s\n", name);
    
    float price1 = 19.99
    float price2 = -1.50

    printf("%+.1f\n",price1);
    printf("%+.3f\n",price2);
    return 0;
}

Output 

Age: 20
Marks: 85.500000
Grade: A
Name: Nova
+20.0
-1.500

Conclusion

I learned how to use different format specifiers to display various data types in C programming. 
This is an important basic concept for understanding input and output operations in C.

